# Atom

An **atom** is a statement taken whole: the smallest thing a logic gives a
truth value to, because the logic does not look inside it. What makes it
atomic is that it is not analysed. Its length has nothing to do with it. A
long sentence of natural language is an atom as soon as it is treated as one
unit that is true or false, and that is what a claim of this vault is.

Seen from its meaning, an atom is a point of a mapping from strings to truth
values:

```
String -> Bool
```

The string is the atom. The mapping is what gives it a value, and nothing in
the string does.

## Description

### Why the vault needs the concept

The vault spoke of formal systems and of atoms before it defined either. They
are needed together and they are different needs.

A [[formal-system]] is a language, axioms, rules and the derivations these
allow. It is what lets something be *proved*. An atom is what the language is
built from, and it is also something a formal system does not require one to
analyse. A person can hold three hundred sentences, give each a value, and ask
which pairs cannot both be true, with no rule of inference in sight. That is
already work with atoms, and it is the work this vault does first.

So the order is: atoms first, as soon as statements are separated and counted;
a formal system later, when rules are added over them. The vault went in that
order.

### Two lanes under one word

The word covers two things, and the vault uses both.

| | Propositional atom | Atomic formula |
|---|---|---|
| What it is | a statement with no inner structure the logic can see | a predicate applied to terms |
| Example | "What makes a loop strange is that it crosses levels of description." | `requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)` |
| Compared with another by | being the same string, or by a judgement of meaning | being the same string, or by rules over its parts |
| What can be asked of it | is it true | is it true, and what does it share with other formulas |
| In a claim | the `text` | the field named `atom` |

Both are atomic in the same sense: neither contains "and", "or", "not" or
"if". They differ in what is inside. The propositional atom is opaque. The
atomic formula has a predicate and arguments, and those are what let two
formulas from different cards meet, or one follow from another.

Every claim of the vault is an atom in the first lane. About two thirds have
also been written in the second. The method's phrase "a claim without an
atom" therefore means a claim that is a propositional atom and has no atomic
formula yet. It does not mean a claim outside the logic.

Passing from the first lane to the second is a [[translation]], out of
natural language. Whether the formula says what the sentence meant is a
judgement. It can be checked by translating back, or by formalising twice and
comparing, and it cannot be proved.

### An atom as a mapping

In two-valued logic a valuation is a function from atoms to `true` and
`false`. Everything else follows from it: the value of "A and B" is computed
from the values of A and B, and the atoms are where the computing stops and
something has to be given.

That is why "a mapping from string to bool" is the right short description.
It also shows what an atom is not. An atom does not carry its truth. Two
valuations can disagree on every atom while the atoms stay the same strings.

The vault changes the mapping in three ways and keeps the atoms.

- **Many sources.** There is one mapping per card, not one for the vault. A
  card gives values only to the atoms it speaks on.
- **Four values.** Collected over the cards, an atom is asserted, denied,
  both, or unsaid. The two extra values are what classical logic has no room
  for: a gap where no card speaks, and a glut where two cards speak against
  each other.
- **Degrees.** Beside the status each claim carries a credence, so the
  mapping is also from strings to numbers between 0 and 1.

### Syntax and meaning

An atom is a piece of syntax, part of the language of a formal system. Its
value is meaning and belongs to no formal system as such. Giving values to
atoms, however carefully, proves nothing. Proof is derivation by rules. The
vault has both, a valuation with four values and many sources and a short
list of rules over the atomic formulas, and they remain two things: an atom
can be derived and denied.

Because atoms are strings, they can themselves be named, counted and encoded.
That is the opening [[godel-numbering]] uses: once statements are objects, a
system can make statements about its own statements. The addresses of the
claims, such as `strange-loop#2`, are a small case of the same move.

## Relations

- It is the unit of the language of a [[formal-system]] {relation: part-of}, and it can be used before any rules exist.
- Writing a natural-language atom as an atomic formula is a [[translation]] {relation: uses} whose result is a judgement.
- Atoms are what [[deduction]] {relation: uses} starts from and does not look into, in the propositional lane.
- Being strings, atoms can be encoded, which is what [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses} does with the formulas of arithmetic.
- A [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: uses} is a formal system whose atoms can be about its own atoms.
- Giving an atom a value is [[evaluation]] {relation: uses} in its sense of assessment, and it is not a proof.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}: it is the first thing the formalism needs.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}: claims read from headers are atoms held as typed data.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[formal-system]].** A language, axioms, rules of inference and the
  derivations they allow. Atoms are the ground of its language.
- **[[translation]].** Carrying a statement from one language into another.
  From a sentence to an atomic formula it leaves natural language, where no
  proof of correctness is available.
- **[[godel-numbering]].** The encoding of formulas as numbers. It depends on
  formulas being finite strings, which atoms are.
- **[[deduction]].** Inference from premises by rules. It adds no atoms of its
  own and rearranges the ones it was given.

## Queries

The atoms of one card in both lanes: the sentence, and the atomic formula
where there is one.

```hql
from claims import claims_of

cards
| filter(c => c.name == "atom")
| flat_map(c => claims_of(c) | map(k => {claim: fmt("{}#{}", c.name, k.id), formula: k.atom.unwrap_or("(none)"), sentence: k.text}))
```

How many claims of the vault are atoms in the first lane only, and how many
in both:

```hql
from claims import claims_of

all = cards | flat_map(claims_of) | List
{claims: size(all), with_formula: all | filter(k => is_some(k.atom)) | size, sentence_only: all | filter(k => is_none(k.atom)) | size}
```

## Sources

- This card does not come from the discussion files. It was asked for when
  the claims were added: the method used the word "atom" for the atomic
  formula only, and the reading given here, that an atom is any statement
  taken whole and is a mapping from a string to a truth value, corrects that.
- The method documents `claims.hmd` and `partial-logic.hmd` in the
  `methodology/` folder, for how atoms are written and compared.
- The proposal "A formal system for claims: from stated to derived" in
  `doc/proposals/`, for what has to be added to atoms before anything is
  proved.
